      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is a Czech Republic OIML certificate R49/2006-CZ-11.01, Revision 1, for Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8705. This is a water meter certificate (R49 = Water meters for cold potable water and hot water).\n\nThe fragment contains three pages (5, 6, 7 of 7), each with a table of characteristics per nominal diameter (DN):\n\n**Page 5**: DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80\n- Q4: ≤ 7.9, ≤ 20.0, ≤ 50.0, ≤ 78.8, ≤ 200 m³/h\n- Q3: ≤ 6.3, ≤ 16.0, ≤ 40.0, ≤ 63.0, ≤ 160 m³/h (footnote 1)\n- Q2: ≥ 0.0504, ≥ 0.128, ≥ 0.320, ≥ 0.504, ≥ 2.56 m³/h\n- Q1: ≥ 0.0320, ≥ 0.080, ≥ 0.200, ≥ 0.315, ≥ 1.60 m³/h\n- Q3/Q1: ≤ 200 (footnote 2) for DN 15-50, ≤ 100 (footnote 2) for DN 80\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 all\n- Q4/Q3: 1.25 all\n- Accuracy class: 2 for DN 15-50, 1 for DN 80\n- MPE lower zone: ±5% (DN 15-50), ±3% (DN 80)\n- MPE upper zone: for water ≤30°C: ±2% (DN 15-50), ±1% (DN 80); for water >30°C: ±3% (DN 15-50), ±2% (DN 80)\n- Temperature classes: T50 for polyurethane, T70 for neoprene, T90 for PTFE liner\n- Environmental classes: B and C\n- Electromagnetic environment: E1 and E2\n- Max admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar\n- Max pressure-loss: 0.16 bar\n- Indicating range: 99 999 to 999 999 m³\n- Resolution of indicating device [L]: 0.1 for DN 15, 25 (colspan 2), 1 for DN 40, 50, 80 (colspan 3)\n- Min straight length inlet: 5\n- Min straight length outlet: 2\n- Orientation: Arbitrary\n- Length of water meter [mm]: 200 for all\n- Connection type: Standard flanges\n- Power supply AC: Umax 250 V, Umin 90 V, Frequency 50 or 60\n- Power supply DC: Umax 42 V, Umin 12 V\n\n**Page 6**: DN 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350\n- Q4: ≤ 313, ≤ 788, ≤ 1250, ≤ 2000, ≤ 3125, ≤ 3125\n- Q3: ≤ 250, ≤ 630, ≤ 1000, ≤ 1600, ≤ 2500, ≤ 2500\n- Q2: ≥ 4.0, ≥ 10.1, ≥ 16.0, ≥ 25.6, ≥ 40.0, ≥ 50.0\n- Q1: ≥ 2.5, ≥ 6.3, ≥ 10.0, ≥ 16.0, ≥ 25.0, ≥ 31.3\n- Q3/Q1: ≤ 100 (footnote 2) for DN 100-300 (colspan 5), ≤ 80 for DN 350\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Q4/Q3: 1.25\n- Accuracy class: 1 (all)\n- MPE lower: ±3%\n- MPE upper: ≤30°C ±1%, >30°C ±2%\n- Temperature classes: T50 polyurethane, T70 neoprene, T90 PTFE\n- Env classes: B and C\n- EM environment: E1 and E2\n- Max admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar\n- Max pressure-loss: 0.16 bar\n- Indicating range: 999 999 to 9 999 999 m³\n- Resolution [L]: 1 for DN 100, 10 for DN 150-300 (colspan 4), 100 for DN 350\n- Min straight lengths: 5 inlet, 2 outlet\n- Orientation: Arbitrary\n- Length [mm]: 250, 300, 350, 435, 512, 588\n- Connection: Standard flanges\n- Power supply: same as page 5\n\n**Page 7**: DN 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900\n- Q4: ≤ 3125, ≤ 3125, ≤ 5000, ≤ 5000, ≤ 7875, ≤ 12500\n- Q3: ≤ 2500, ≤ 2500, ≤ 4000, ≤ 4000, ≤ 6300, ≤ 10000\n- Q2: ≥ 63.5, ≥ 80.0, ≥ 101.6, ≥ 128.0, ≥ 201.6, ≥ 320.0\n- Q1: ≥ 39.7, ≥ 50.0, ≥ 63.5, ≥ 80.0, ≥ 126.0, ≥ 200.0\n- Q3/Q1: ≤63, ≤50, ≤63, then colspan 3: ≤50 for DN 600, 750, 900\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Q4/Q3: 1.25\n- Accuracy class: 1\n- MPE lower: ±3%\n- MPE upper: ≤30°C ±1%, >30°C ±2%\n- Temperature classes: same\n- Env classes: B and C\n- EM: E1 and E2\n- Max admissible pressure: 0 to 40 bar\n- Max pressure-loss: 0.16 bar\n- Indicating range: 9 999 999 (hmm, just one value — single value, weird; likely 9 999 999 m³ fixed)\n- Resolution: 100 L\n- Min straight lengths: 5, 2\n- Orientation: Arbitrary\n- Length [mm]: 664, 761, 839, 1000, 1200, 1200\n- Connection: Standard flanges\n- Power supply: same\n\nFootnotes:\n1) The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\n2) The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\n\nSo this is a water meter family with 17 models (DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900).\n\nNow let me structure the JSON.\n\nSince this fragment doesn't have the certificate header info (no issuing authority, applicants, etc. — those are presumably on page 1 which is part 1 of the markdown), I should only include what's present:\n\n- certificate: number (R49/2006-CZ-11.01), member_state (Czech Republic), revision. Hmm, the schema has `certificate` with fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. There's no revision field in the schema but revision_history exists. Actually the schema has `revision_history[]` with revision, date, changes. Here we just have \"Revision 1\" with no date/changes. I could include revision_history with revision \"1\" and no date... but rule 8 says don't fabricate. I could add revision: \"1\". Hmm, but the schema for revision_history requires revision, date, changes. Since date and changes aren't present, maybe omit or include with nulls. I think I'll include `certificate` with number and member_state. Maybe include page_total: 7? \"This certificates includes 7 pages\" — yes, page_total: 7. Actually the fragment says \"Page 5 This certificates includes 7 pages\" etc. So page_total is 7. But wait — the fragment only shows pages 5-7. The schema field page_total refers to document-level total, which is 7. I'll include it.\n\nActually, wait — should I include certificate.number? The certificate number is present in the fragment: \"R49/2006-CZ-11.01\". Yes.\n\n- recommendation: R49. The certificate number starts with R49/2006-CZ-11.01, so the recommendation is R49 (Water meters), edition... Hmm, \"R49/2006\" — R49 was published in 2006? Actually OIML R49 edition 2006? The 2006 in the certificate number is part of the Czech numbering scheme \"R49/2006-CZ-11.01\". Typically Czech certs are numbered like R49/2006-CZ1-11.01. The 2006 might refer to the year. Should I infer edition 2006? The schema says edition (int). I could put edition 2006 based on the numbering. Hmm, risky. But conventionally in OIML-CS, the certificate number format is R{rec}/{year}-{country}-{serial}. So R49 edition 2006. Actually OIML R49 was published 2006 (edition 2006? Actually R49-1:2013 exists... but R49 2006 edition exists too). I think it's safe to include recommendation.id \"R49\" and edition 2006. Hmm, but rule 8: don't fabricate. The \"2006\" is in the certificate number. I'll include id: \"R49\", edition: 2006. Accuracy classes: 2 and 1 (from the tables). Include accuracy_classes: [2, 1]. Hmm — accuracy class \"2\" for DN 15-50 and \"1\" for DN 80-900. Yes.\n\nActually, wait — accuracy classes as numbers [1, 2]. R49 accuracy classes are 1, 2 (class 1 and class 2). I'll include [2, 1] or [1, 2]. I'll do [1, 2] sorted.\n\nHmm, should I be careful? The recommendation block should only be included if present. The certificate number implies R49. I think including it is reasonable and helpful. But strictly \"don't fabricate\" — the ID R49 is directly derived from the cert number. I'll include it with edition 2006 derived from cert number. Actually, I'm a bit torn. Let me include it since it's clearly inferable.\n\n- certified_type: category — this is a water meter (R49). The title says \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8705\". Category could be \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters). type_designations: [\"8732\", \"8705\"]. Components: transmitter type 8732, sensor type 8705. Since the cert names sub-assemblies (transmitter and sensor), I should include components[]:\n  - role: \"Transmitter\" (Measuring transmitter?), type_designations: [\"8732\"]\n  - role: \"Sensor\" (Measuring sensor?), type_designations: [\"8705\"]\n  \n  Actually magnetic flow meter = sensor + transmitter. The transmitter is the calculating/indicating device; the sensor is the measuring sensor. I'll use roles \"Transmitter\" and \"Sensor\".\n\n- model_family: models per DN. 17 models. Model IDs: \"DN15\", \"DN25\", ..., \"DN900\". Or \"DN 15\". I'll use \"DN15\" etc.\n\n- characteristics: \n  - type_level: attributes common to all models: \n    - ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n    - ratio_q4_over_q3: 1.25\n    - environmental_classes: \"B and C\" → value \"B\", \"C\"? Hmm — \"B and C\" is a list of classes. I could use value: [\"B\", \"C\"]? The schema value can be number | {min,max} | string. A list isn't allowed. I'll use string \"B and C\" or value: \"B, C\". I'll use \"B and C\".\n    - electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\"\n    - temperature_classes: \"T50 for polyurethane liner, T70 for neoprene liner, T90 for PTFE liner\" — this varies by lining material (config axis), not by model. I could put as type_level string or config_level. Maybe config_level with axis \"lining_material\": T50→polyurethane, T70→neoprene, T90→PTFE. That fits config_level nicely. Hmm, but temperature class is an attribute whose value depends on the lining material config. config_level: attribute \"temperature_class\", axis \"lining_material\", values: [{condition: \"polyurethane\", value: \"T50\"}, {condition: \"neoprene\", value: \"T70\"}, {condition: \"PTFE\", value: \"T90\"}]. That's good.\n    - min straight length inlet: 5 (dimensionless — ratio to DN)\n    - min straight length outlet: 2\n    - orientation: \"Arbitrary orientation\"\n    - connection_type: \"Standard flanges\"\n    - power supply: type AC, Umax 250 V, Umin 90 V, frequency 50 or 60; type DC, Umax 42 V, Umin 12 V. These are two alternatives. I could model as config_level with axis \"power_supply_type\": AC/DC with voltage ranges. Or type_level with labels ac_max_voltage etc. Let me use config_level:\n      - attribute: \"supply_voltage\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: {min: 90, max: 250}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: {min: 12, max: 42}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\n      - attribute: \"power_supply_frequency\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: null}] — hmm, DC frequency is not applicable. Better: only AC condition, or type_level ac_power_supply_frequency {min:50,max:60} Hz. Hmm.\n      \n      Alternatively, use type_level with canonical labels: ac_max_voltage (250 V), ac_min_voltage (90 V), ac_frequency ({min 50, max 60} Hz), dc_max_voltage (42 V), dc_min_voltage (12 V). That's straightforward. But config_level is designed for this. The rules example 3: \"110-240V AC 50/60Hz → SPLIT into power_supply_voltage, power_supply_frequency, power_supply_type\". Here there are two supply types. I'll use config_level for voltage with axis \"power_supply_type\", and put frequency as AC-only config. Actually simpler and safer: type_level with distinct labels:\n      - power_supply_type_ac: \"AC\" — redundant.\n      \n      Hmm. Let me think about what's cleanest:\n      \n      config_level entries:\n      - {attribute: \"maximum_supply_voltage_umax\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: 250, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: 42, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]}\n      - {attribute: \"minimum_supply_voltage_umin\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: 90, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]}\n      - {attribute: \"supply_frequency\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: null, unit_symbol: null}]}\n      \n      Hmm, \"value\": null — the schema says value is number|{min,max}|string. null might violate. For DC, frequency doesn't apply. I could just omit DC condition for frequency, making it implicitly AC-only. That's cleaner: values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}].\n      \n      Wait, but what does the structured value look like in config_level? The schema says: `config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}`. Rule 5 says values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. Hmm, actually the rule says \"Values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`\" — so value should itself be the structured object? Ambiguous. Looking at rule 5: \"use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" I think {condition, value: <structured value>, unit_symbol} or {condition, value: {value, unit_symbol}}? Given model_level example: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — the value there is plain, with unit at the attribute level. For config_level, I'll do {condition, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers} where value is the plain number/range/string. That seems most consistent.\n\n  - model_level: attributes varying by model:\n    - nominal_diameter (DN) [mm]\n    - overload_flowrate_q4 [m³/h] — values are upper bounds (≤). Should value be {min: null, max: X}? The rule says ≤2.5 → {min: null, max: 2.5}. So yes, {min: null, max: 7.9} etc. Hmm, but these are flowrate ranges: Q4 is the maximum overload flowrate. The \"≤\" indicates Q4 ≤ 7.9, i.e., maximum overload flowrate is 7.9 m³/h. For a characteristic \"overload flowrate q4\", the value being \"≤ 7.9\" means the maximum permitted. I'll encode as {min: null, max: 7.9}. Alternatively value: 7.9 with the understanding it's a max. I think following the rule: open-ended ranges use null. So {min: null, max: 7.9}.\n    \n    Hmm, actually for flowrates like Q3 ≤ 6.3 — this is \"permanent flowrate is at most 6.3 m³/h\" i.e., the rated value chosen from R5 series. It's effectively a max. I'll use {min: null, max: X}.\n    \n    - permanent_flowrate_q3 [m³/h], footnote_markers: [\"1)\"]\n    - transitional_flowrate_q2 [m³/h] — {min: X, max: null} (≥ values)\n    - minimum_flowrate_q1 [m³/h] — {min: X, max: null}\n    - ratio_q3_over_q1 — varies: DN15: ≤200, DN25: ≤200, DN40: ≤200, DN50: ≤200, DN80: ≤100; DN100-300: ≤100, DN350: ≤80; DN400: ≤63, DN450: ≤50, DN500: ≤63, DN600: ≤50, DN750: ≤50, DN900: ≤50. footnote 2. Encode as {min: null, max: 200} etc. with footnote_markers [\"2)\"].\n    - accuracy_class: 2 for DN15-50, 1 for DN80-900.\n    - mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: ±5% for DN15-50 (class 2), ±3% for DN80-900 (class 1). value: -5 to 5? \"±5%\" → value could be 5 with unit \"%\". I'll use value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\" and label mpe_lower_zone. Or {min: -5, max: 5}. I think simpler: value: 5, unit: \"%\". Hmm, ± means the MPE magnitude. I'll use 5 with unit \"%\". Actually to be safe and lossless, maybe value: {min: -5, max: 5}? That's awkward. The common practice: value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\". I'll go with that. Label: \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\" (mpe_lower_zone). \n    - mpe upper zone: two values depending on water temperature: ≤30°C → ±2%/±1%; >30°C → ±3%/±2%. And it varies by accuracy class: class 2 (DN15-50): ±2% (≤30°C), ±3% (>30°C); class 1 (DN80+): ±1%, ±2%. So this is config_level: attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_zone\", axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: ...}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: ...}] — but the value varies per model too (5/3 for class 2, 1/2 for class 1... wait: class 2: MPEl ±5%, MPEu ±2% (≤30), ±3% (>30). Class 1: MPEl ±3%, MPEu ±1%, ±2%).\n    \n    Hmm. So the MPE values depend on accuracy class which depends on model. Options:\n    - model_level for mpe entries with per-model values. mpe_upper_zone has two sub-conditions (temperature). I could add config_level with axis \"water_temperature\" and then have condition strings that include class? Messy.\n    - Simplify: put mpe_upper_zone in config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\": [{condition: \"class 2\", ...}] — no, temperature is the axis in the table.\n    \n    Alternative: create model_level attributes:\n    - mpe_lower_zone: values per model (5 for DN15-50; 3 for DN80+), unit \"%\"\n    - mpe_upper_zone_water_temp_le_30c: per model (2 for DN15-50; 1 for DN80+), unit \"%\"\n    - mpe_upper_zone_water_temp_gt_30c: per model (3 for DN15-50; 2 for DN80+), unit \"%\"\n    \n    That's explicit but splits the temperature axis into attribute names. Alternatively, use config_level with a combined axis: axis \"water_temperature\", and conditions \"≤ 30 °C\" / \"> 30 °C\", but then value can't vary per model. Since value differs across models, config_level alone can't capture it.\n\n    I think the cleanest per the schema: model_level for mpe values split by temperature condition as separate attributes. Hmm, but maybe better: put them in config_level where the axis is \"water_temperature\" and conditions embed the class... no.\n\n    Actually another approach: config_level entries with axis \"accuracy_class\":\n    - attribute: mpe_lower_zone, axis: accuracy_class, values: [{condition: \"2\", value: 5, unit \"%\"}, {condition: \"1\", value: 3, unit \"%\"}]\n    - attribute: mpe_upper_zone, axis: \"accuracy_class × water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"2, ≤30°C\", value: 2}, {condition: \"2, >30°C\", value: 3}, {condition: \"1, ≤30°C\", value: 1}, {condition: \"1, >30°C\", value: 2}]\n    \n    That's a bit hacky with compound conditions. Hmm.\n\n    I think I'll go with model_level split attributes — it's unambiguous:\n    - \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\": values per model\n    - \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_30c_or_below\": per model\n    - \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_above_30c\": per model\n    \n    Hmm wait, but maybe cleaner: use config_level for temperature axis and put per-class values into... no, config_level values are condition→value single mapping.\n\n    Actually, reconsider: accuracy_class itself is model_level. And MPEs are determined by class per R49: class 2: MPEu = ±2%/±3%, MPEl = ±5%; class 1: MPEu = ±1%/±2%, MPEl = ±3%. The cert just restates them. \n\n    Decision: I'll put accuracy_class in model_level, and MPE entries in config_level with axis \"water_temperature\" AND separately... no.\n\n    OK let me just go with model_level for the three MPE attributes (split by temperature for upper zone). Also mpe_lower could be derived from accuracy class but I'll include it as model_level too.\n\n    Actually wait — maybe I can use config_level with axis \"water_temperature\" where the conditions are per accuracy class since the table on page 6/7 lists MPEu as one colspan cell \"±1% ≤30°C, ±2% >30°C\" for all models in that table, and page 5 has \"±2%/±3%\" for DN15-50 and \"±1%/±2%\" for DN80. So a combined approach: config_level with axis \"accuracy_class_and_water_temperature\"? Too hacky.\n\n    Final: model_level:\n    - nominal_diameter\n    - overload_flowrate_q4\n    - permanent_flowrate_q3 (footnote 1)\n    - transitional_flowrate_q2\n    - minimum_flowrate_q1\n    - ratio_q3_over_q1 (footnote 2, as max bounds)\n    - accuracy_class\n    - mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\n    - mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_le_30c\n    - mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_gt_30c\n    - length_of_water_meter [mm] — page 5: 200 all; page 6: varies 250,300,350,435,512,588; page 7: 664,761,839,1000,1200,1200\n    - resolution_of_indicating_device [L]: DN15: 0.1, DN25: 0.1, DN40: 1, DN50: 1, DN80: 1; DN100: 1, DN150: 10, DN200: 10, DN250: 10, DN300: 10, DN350: 100; DN400-900: 100.\n    \n    Wait let me re-check page 5 resolution: `<td>Resolution of the indicating device [L]:</td><td colspan=\"2\">0.1</td><td colspan=\"3\">1</td>` — 5 columns total: DN 15, 25 → 0.1; DN 40, 50, 80 → 1. Yes.\n    \n    Page 6: `<td>1</td><td colspan=\"4\">10</td><td>100</td>` — DN 100 → 1; DN 150,200,250,300 → 10; DN 350 → 100. Yes.\n    \n    Page 7: all 100.\n    \n    - max admissible pressure: page 5: 0 to 50 bar; page 6: 0 to 50 bar; page 7: 0 to 40 bar. So it varies! DN15-350: {min: 0, max: 50}; DN400-900: {min: 0, max: 40}. So model_level attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with unit bar. Actually \"0 to 50\" is a range — pressure range. Value {min: 0, max: 50}. Per model.\n    - max pressure-loss: 0.16 bar everywhere → type_level.\n    - indicating range [m³]: page 5: \"99 999 to 999 999\" → {min: 99999, max: 999999}; page 6: \"999 999 to 9 999 999\" → {min: 999999, max: 9999999}; page 7: \"9 999 999\" → single value 9999999. Hmm. So model_level \"indicating_range\" with unit \"m³\". For page 7, value 9999999 (single number — maybe it's max only? The indicating range is just 9 999 999, odd but that's what it says). I'll encode as value: 9999999 for those, or {min: null, max: 9999999}? The literal is \"9 999 999\" a single number. I'll use value: 9999999.\n\n- matrix_tables: I should preserve the three per-page tables. Each table is DN × attributes. The schema example shows matrix_tables with name, columns, rows (object per row), description, footnotes. I'll create three matrix_tables entries:\n  1. \"Characteristics for DN 15–80\" columns: nominal_diameter, overload_flowrate_q4, permanent_flowrate_q3, transitional_flowrate_q2, minimum_flowrate_q1, ratio_q3_over_q1, accuracy_class, mpe_lower_flowrate_zone, mpe_upper_flowrate_zone, temperature_class, maximum_admissible_pressure, maximum_pressure_loss, indicating_range, resolution, length_of_water_meter...\n  \n  Hmm, that's a lot. Maybe I should represent rows as DNs with the main numeric columns, and description for the rest. Or include the full set of columns with mixed types. The example shows columns with units separately: \"column_units\". Wait, in the example there's \"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", ...]. The schema yaml lists \"name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]\" but the example JSON includes column_units. I'll include column_units too.\n\n  I'll build three tables:\n  \n  Table 1 (page 5, DN 15–80):\n  columns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"ratio_q2_over_q1\", \"ratio_q4_over_q3\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_le_30c\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_gt_30c\", \"maximum_admissible_pressure\", \"maximum_pressure_loss\", \"indicating_range\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\", \"length_of_water_meter\"]\n  \n  Hmm, that's very wide. Maybe trim to the key numeric columns and mention the rest in description. But being lossless is good. Let me include the essential columns: dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, resolution, length. And put the common stuff in type_level. MPE values are also per-DN so maybe include them.\n\n  Actually, since model_level already captures the per-model matrix, matrix_tables serve as preservation. I'll include them with the main columns.\n\n  For values in matrix_tables rows — should they be structured objects or plain? The example shows plain: {\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...}. So plain values with column_units. For \"≤ 7.9\" I'd store 7.9 (the number) — losing the ≤. Hmm. In the R137 example, values like qmax 4.0 are stored plainly. I'll store numeric values, and where the original is a bound (≤/≥) I'll... hmm. In model_level I use structured {min: null, max: X}. In matrix_tables I could keep it simple with the numeric value since column semantics (\"overload flowrate Q4 [m³/h] ≤\") — the ≤ is in the row label. I could note in description that q4/q3 are upper bounds and q1/q2 are lower bounds. Or encode strings \"≤ 7.9\". The example uses plain numbers. I'll use numbers and add a description noting bounds. Actually, to be safe I'll just use the numbers — model_level preserves the bounds semantics via min/max objects.\n\n  Hmm, wait. Actually, do I even need matrix_tables if model_level captures everything? The R137 example shows both. This R49 case is like R137 (explicit 2-D tables). So yes, include matrix_tables AND model_level. I'll include all three tables.\n\nLet me now decide on value encodings for model_level:\n\nModel list (17):\n1. DN15\n2. DN25\n3. DN40\n4. DN50\n5. DN80\n6. DN100\n7. DN150\n8. DN200\n9. DN250\n10. DN300\n11. DN350\n12. DN400\n13. DN450\n14. DN500\n15. DN600\n16. DN750\n17. DN900\n\nnominal_diameter [mm]: 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900.\n\noverload_flowrate_q4 [m³/h] (upper bounds):\n- DN15: 7.9\n- DN25: 20.0 → 20\n- DN40: 50.0 → 50\n- DN50: 78.8\n- DN80: 200\n- DN100: 313\n- DN150: 788\n- DN200: 1250\n- DN250: 2000\n- DN300: 3125\n- DN350: 3125\n- DN400: 3125\n- DN450: 3125\n- DN500: 5000\n- DN600: 5000\n- DN750: 7875\n- DN900: 12500\n\npermanent_flowrate_q3 [m³/h] (upper bounds, footnote 1):\n- 6.3, 16, 40, 63, 160, 250, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 2500, 2500, 4000, 4000, 6300, 10000\n\ntransitional_flowrate_q2 [m³/h] (lower bounds):\n- 0.0504, 0.128, 0.32, 0.504, 2.56, 4.0, 10.1, 16.0, 25.6, 40.0, 50.0, 63.5, 80.0, 101.6, 128.0, 201.6, 320.0\n\nminimum_flowrate_q1 [m³/h] (lower bounds):\n- 0.032, 0.08, 0.2, 0.315, 1.6, 2.5, 6.3, 10.0, 16.0, 25.0, 31.3, 39.7, 50.0, 63.5, 80.0, 126.0, 200.0\n\nratio_q3_over_q1 (upper bounds, footnote 2):\n- DN15: 200, DN25: 200, DN40: 200, DN50: 200, DN80: 100\n- DN100: 100, DN150: 100, DN200: 100, DN250: 100, DN300: 100, DN350: 80\n- DN400: 63, DN450: 50, DN500: 63, DN600: 50, DN750: 50, DN900: 50\n\naccuracy_class:\n- DN15: 2, DN25: 2, DN40: 2, DN50: 2, DN80: 1, then all 1.\n\nmpe_lower_flowrate_zone [%]:\n- DN15: 5, DN25: 5, DN40: 5, DN50: 5, DN80: 3, rest 3.\n\nmpe_upper_zone ≤30°C [%]:\n- DN15-50: 2; DN80+: 1.\n\nmpe_upper_zone >30°C [%]:\n- DN15-50: 3; DN80+: 2.\n\nmaximum_admissible_pressure [bar] (range):\n- DN15-350: {min: 0, max: 50}\n- DN400-900: {min: 0, max: 40}\n\nindicating_range [m³]:\n- DN15-80: {min: 99999, max: 999999}\n- DN100-350: {min: 999999, max: 9999999}\n- DN400-900: 9999999 (single value)\n\nresolution_of_indicating_device [L]:\n- DN15: 0.1, DN25: 0.1, DN40: 1, DN50: 1, DN80: 1, DN100: 1, DN150: 10, DN200: 10, DN250: 10, DN300: 10, DN350: 100, DN400: 100, DN450: 100, DN500: 100, DN600: 100, DN750: 100, DN900: 100.\n\nlength_of_water_meter [mm]:\n- DN15: 200, DN25: 200, DN40: 200, DN50: 200, DN80: 200\n- DN100: 250, DN150: 300, DN200: 350, DN250: 435, DN300: 512, DN350: 588\n- DN400: 664, DN450: 761, DN500: 839, DN600: 1000, DN750: 1200, DN900: 1200\n\ntype_level:\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- environmental_classes: \"B and C\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\"\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 5 (dimensionless — it's in DN multiples)\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 2\n- orientation_limitation: \"Arbitrary orientation\"\n- connection_type: \"Standard flanges\"\n- maximum_pressure_loss: 0.16 bar\n- power supply stuff.\n\nConfig level:\n- temperature_class by lining_material: T50 polyurethane, T70 neoprene, T90 PTFE.\n- power supply: AC: Umax 250 V, Umin 90 V, freq 50 or 60; DC: Umax 42 V, Umin 12 V.\n\nFor power supply, config_level:\n- attribute: \"maximum_supply_voltage_umax\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: 250, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: 42, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\n- attribute: \"minimum_supply_voltage_umin\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: 90, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\n- attribute: \"supply_frequency\", axis: \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}, {condition: \"DC\", value: null, unit_symbol: null}] — hmm, \"value\": null — the schema says value is number|{min,max}|string. null might violate. For DC, frequency doesn't apply. I could just omit DC condition for frequency, making it implicitly AC-only. That's cleaner: values: [{condition: \"AC\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}].\n\nWait, but what does the structured value look like in config_level? The schema says: `config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}`. Rule 5 says values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. Hmm, actually the rule says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\" — so value should itself be the structured object? Ambiguous. Looking at rule 5: \"use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" I think {condition, value: <structured value>, unit_symbol} or {condition, value: {value, unit_symbol}}? Given model_level example: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — the value there is plain, with unit at the attribute level. For config_level, I'll do {condition, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers} where value is the plain number/range/string. That seems most consistent.\n\nFootnotes:\n- \"1)\": \"The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\"\n- \"2)\": \"The ratio Q3 / Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\"\n\nMarker format: The rule example shows `\"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]` — with the parenthesis. The superscripts are \"1\" and \"2\". I'll use \"1)\" and \"2)\".\n\ncomponents:\n- {role: \"Transmitter\", type_designations: [\"8732\"]} — full name \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter\". Maybe role: \"Measuring transmitter\"? The cert says \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732\". I'll do role: \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter\", type_designations: [\"8732\"]. And role: \"Magnetic Flow Sensor\", type_designations: [\"8705\"]. Hmm, keep roles simple: \"Transmitter\" and \"Sensor\"? I'll use descriptive: \"Magnetic flow meter transmitter\" and \"Magnetic flow sensor\". Actually schema: role, type_designations. I'll use \"Transmitter\" / \"Sensor\" for canonical simplicity but include full names... there's no name field. I'll use role: \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter\", type_designations: [\"8732\"] and role: \"Magnetic Flow Sensor\", type_designations: [\"8705\"]. Also note lining materials: Neoprene, Polyurethane, PTFE — that's a characteristic of the sensor/liner. Could add characteristics to the sensor component: lining_material options. I'll add characteristics: {\"lining_materials\": [\"Neoprene\", \"Polyurethane\", \"PTFE\"]} to the sensor component. Characteristics{} is free-form.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (R49). Hmm, the title says \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8705\". Category options given: \"Measuring system\", etc. R49 is water meters, and the tables mention \"Length of water meter\". So category \"Water meter\". Actually is a magnetic flow meter a water meter? Yes, electromagnetic water meters fall under R49. I'll use \"Water meter\". Hmm, could also be \"Flow meter\". The R49 recommendation is \"Water meters for cold potable water and hot water\". I'll go with \"Water meter\" since cert is R49 and rows say \"Length of water meter\". Maybe safer: \"Water meter (electromagnetic flow meter)\"? Keep it simple: \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"8732\", \"8705\"]\n- module_designation: hmm, R49 doesn't use modules like R60/R76. Omit or null. I'll omit.\n- description: \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8705 with the lining material made by Neoprene, Polyurethane and PTFE\" — this is the title of the cert, a description. Include it.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2006-CZ-11.01\"\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- page_total: 7\n- scheme: R49 cert — scheme A or B? Not stated in this fragment. Omit.\n- Revision: I could note in revision_history: [{revision: \"1\"}] — but date and changes unknown. The schema shows revision, date, changes. Including with nulls? Rule 8: don't fabricate — I can include revision \"1\" and omit date/changes. I think it's acceptable to include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\"}]. Hmm, \"Revision 1\" is present in the fragment. I'll include it with just the revision field. Actually, is Revision 1 the current revision? Yes, the certificate is Revision 1. I'll include.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006, accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Should scheme be included? Not stated. Omit.\n\nHmm, about edition: \"R49/2006-CZ-11.01\" — the \"2006\" could be the year of issue rather than edition. Czech OIML-CS certs: format is R{number}/{year}-{country}-{sequence}. E.g., \"R60/2000-CZ1-98.01\". The year is typically the recommendation edition year or issue year. OIML R49:1995? R49 was published 1995? Hmm. Actually OIML R 49 \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water\" was 1995? No wait. R49-1:2013, R49-2:2013 exist. There was R49:1995? Hmm. Hmm, actually R49:1995? Let me think. The 2004 revision: R49-1:2004? Actually I recall OIML R49-1:2013 \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water Part 1: Metrological and technical requirements\". Earlier: R49:1995? There's R72:1984 \"Hot water meters\". Hmm, R49 might be from 1995... but certificate says 2006 issue year maybe. The Czech certificate numbers from CMI typically: \"R49/2006-CZ1-11.01\" where 2006 is the year of the recommendation edition... Given accuracy class table format (Q3/Q4, Q1/Q2, Q2/Q1=1.6, Q4/Q3=1.25, R5/R10 ISO 3:1973), this matches ISO 4064:1999/OIML R49:2000-ish. Actually OIML R49:2000? Hmm. There IS an OIML R49:2000? No... Hmm. I believe R49 \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water\" was published in 1995? Hmm, no. Actually I don't think the exact edition matters — the safest: id \"R49\", edition 2006 (from the number). Since the instruction says don't fabricate, but the number itself contains \"2006\". Given the R60 example \"R60/2000-CZ-...\" convention, edition = year in cert number. I'll include edition: 2006.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether to include recommendation at all. The fragment doesn't explicitly name the recommendation except via the cert number prefix \"R49\". I think it's a reasonable inference, and the extractor's purpose is canonicalization. I'll include id: \"R49\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nHmm, one concern: accuracy_classes — the values are \"2\" and \"1\" — numbers. Fine.\n\nNow model IDs: \"DN15\" ... \"DN900\". Good.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null (no explicit family name). \n- models: 17 entries with model_id \"DN15\" etc.\n\nShould model labels differ? e.g., label \"DN 15\". I'll add label maybe. Keep model_id: \"DN15\".\n\nNow characteristics.model_level entries. Each: attribute, unit_symbol, values[]. Optionally footnote_markers at value level.\n\nLet me define:\n1. nominal_diameter, unit \"mm\", values: 15...900.\n2. overload_flowrate_q4, unit \"m³/h\", values {min: null, max: X}.\n3. permanent_flowrate_q3, unit \"m³/h\", values {min: null, max: X}, footnote_markers [\"1)\"] at each value? Or at attribute level? Schema shows footnote_markers on value objects. I'll put [\"1)\"] on each value for q3 and [\"2)\"] for ratio q3/q1. Hmm, that's verbose but correct. Actually model_level attribute doesn't have footnote_markers field in schema; values have. I'll include per value.\n\nActually wait — should q3 values be {min: null, max: 6.3}? Q3 is \"permanent flowrate ≤ 6.3\" meaning the nominal Q3 rating is at most 6.3. Encoding as upper bound max: 6.3. OK.\n\nSimilarly q4, q3: max bounds; q2, q1: min bounds.\n\n4. transitional_flowrate_q2, unit \"m³/h\", values {min: X, max: null}.\n5. minimum_flowrate_q1, unit \"m³/h\", values {min: X, max: null}.\n6. ratio_q3_over_q1, unit null, values {min: null, max: X}, footnote [\"2)\"].\n7. accuracy_class, unit null, values 2 or 1.\n8. mpe_lower_flowrate_zone, unit \"%\", values 5 or 3. Hmm — should I express as {min: -5, max: 5}? I'll use plain 5 with unit \"%\" — the ± is implied by MPE. Hmm, but lossless? The label says maximum permissible error, ±5%. I think value 5, unit \"%\" is fine and standard.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe put MPE in config_level keyed on accuracy class, since the table ties them: For class 2: MPEl ±5%, MPEu ±2% (≤30°C), ±3% (>30°C). For class 1: MPEl ±3%, MPEu ±1%, ±2%. Using config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" for MPEl and axis \"accuracy_class × water_temperature\"... nah, too hacky. Keep model_level. Actually — alternative: keep MPE in matrix_tables only (they're in the tables), and in model_level include them too for completeness. I'll include in model_level with split attributes for the upper zone. Labels:\n- \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\"\n- \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_30c_and_below\" — canonical snake_case: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_le_30c\". Contains \"le\" — fine, snake_case.\n- \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_gt_30c\".\n\nHmm, alternatively express the upper-zone MPE as config_level with axis \"water_temperature\" and conditions \"≤ 30 °C\" and \"> 30 °C\" — but then values can't be per-model. Unless I make the attribute per class: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\" with axis \"water_temperature\" — no.\n\nOK going with model_level split attributes.\n\n9. maximum_admissible_pressure, unit \"bar\", values {min: 0, max: 50} for DN15-350, {min: 0, max: 40} for DN400-900.\n10. indicating_range, unit \"m³\", values {min: 99999, max: 999999} / {min: 999999, max: 9999999} / 9999999.\n11. resolution_of_indicating_device, unit \"L\", values per model.\n12. length_of_water_meter, unit \"mm\", values per model.\n\ntype_level:\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: {value: 1.25, ...}\n- environmental_classes: {value: \"B and C\"} — hmm, maybe better value: \"B, C\"? Keep \"B and C\" as literal.\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E1 and E2\"}\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: {value: 5, unit_symbol: null} — it's \"5\" (times DN). Dimensionless ratio. unit null.\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: {value: 2}\n- orientation_limitation: {value: \"Arbitrary orientation\"}\n- connection_type: {value: \"Standard flanges\"}\n- maximum_pressure_loss: {value: 0.16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n\nconfig_level:\n1. temperature_class: axis \"lining_material\", values:\n   - {condition: \"polyurethane liner\", value: \"T50\"}\n   - {condition: \"neoprene liner\", value: \"T70\"}\n   - {condition: \"PTFE liner\", value: \"T90\"}\n   Unit null. (Page 5 says \"T70 for neoprene\", pages 6-7 \"T70 for neoprene liner\" — same meaning.)\n2. maximum_supply_voltage (umax): axis \"power_supply_type\": AC→250 V, DC→42 V.\n3. minimum_supply_voltage (umin): axis \"power_supply_type\": AC→90 V, DC→12 V.\n4. supply_frequency: axis \"power_supply_type\": AC→{min 50, max 60} Hz. For DC, not applicable — omit condition.\n   Labels: \"maximum_supply_voltage_umax\", \"minimum_supply_voltage_umin\", \"supply_frequency\". Hmm, maybe \"ac_...\"? The axis captures it. OK.\n\nmatrix_tables: three tables.\n\nTable 1: name \"Nominal diameters DN 15–80 (page 5)\". columns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_le_30c\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temperature_gt_30c\", \"maximum_admissible_pressure\", \"indicating_range\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\", \"length_of_water_meter\"]. column_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, \"%\", \"%\", \"%\", \"bar\", \"m³\", \"L\", \"mm\"].\n\nHmm wait — maximum_admissible_pressure is constant within table 1 (0 to 50), indicating_range constant. Should they be columns? They're in the table as colspan cells. For fidelity I could include them as columns with same value. It makes rows wide. Alternatively note in description. I'll include them as columns since the tables include them (colspan across). Actually, let me keep matrix tables to the columns that genuinely vary per row within the table, and put the rest into description. Hmm... The purpose of matrix_tables is preserving multi-dim tables. Including constant columns is harmless and more faithful. I'll include: dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, mpe_l, mpe_u_le30, mpe_u_gt30, resolution, length. And descr